---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portrait from '../assets/art/catherine-portrait.webp';
---
<Base title="About" description="Who is doing all this annotating.">
  <PageHead eyebrow="About" title="Meet the annotator" note="hi, it's me" />
  <div class="wrap about">
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every book I own has been argued with. There are question marks next to plot holes, hearts next to
        perfect sentences, and at least one "absolutely not" in the margin of a chapter I still think about.
      </p>
      <p>This blog is those margins, moved somewhere other people can read them.</p>
      <p>
        I'm Catherine. By day I teach high school English, which means I spend a lot of time convincing
        fifteen-year-olds that the curtains might actually be blue for a reason. I'm also a certified reading
        specialist, a doctoral student at Texas A&amp;M University (gig 'em), and a writer who can't stop making up stories about small
        creatures with big opinions. This is me taking a big step, because while I love writing, I never share
        it with anyone.
      </p>
      <h2>What lives here</h2>
      <p>
        Honest book reviews that actually take a side. Essays that take the long way around. Lessons and close
        reading for anyone who misses English class (or never got the good version of it). Dinner table ideas
        for parents who want more than "how was school?" "Fine." And my own short stories, because the
        characters won't leave me alone.
      </p>
      <h2>Read it twice</h2>
      <p>
        A lot of my stories have an annotated edition. Read the story first, cold. Then click "Read it again,
        annotated" and the margins fill in with notes on what I was up to. Some of them hide clues. Some just
        show their work. Either way, a good story should be better the second time.
      </p>
      <h2>About the crow</h2>
      <p>
        Why a crow? Crows are clever, they collect shiny things, and they poke at everything to see how it
        works. So do I. I question everything.
      </p>
      <blockquote><p>Read slowly. Argue with the author. Leave evidence that you were here.</p></blockquote>
      <p>Pull up a chair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="art-side">
      <Image src={portrait} alt="A pencil-sketch portrait of Catherine Sauer, smiling, in glasses, inside an ornate oval frame." width={600} />
      <ul class="portrait-notes hand">
        <li class="arrow">↑ that's me</li>
        <li>42 and still doing homework</li>
        <li>teaching under 5 years <span>(cried on day one. stayed.)</span></li>
        <li>two teenagers <span>(pray for me)</span></li>
        <li>one husband, navy veteran <span>(29 years. he signed up for this.)</span></li>
        <li class="last">my life is crazy. sleep is a rumor.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 300px;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .about .prose {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .art-side :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .portrait-notes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    padding: 0 0.4rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    line-height: 1.1;
    transform: rotate(-2deg);
  }
  .portrait-notes li {
    margin: 0 0 0.45rem;
  }
  .portrait-notes li span {
    display: block;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .portrait-notes .arrow {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .portrait-notes .last {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    text-decoration: underline wavy;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
    .art-side {
      max-width: 260px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      order: -1;
    }
  }
</style>
